# EKPO — SEQUENCES VIII-X AWARD-LEVEL REWRITE CANDIDATE

Status: SCREENPLAY CANDIDATE — NOT YET AUTHORITATIVE
Purpose: Replace current Sequences VIII-X after audit approval
Continuity start: Immediately after Sequence VII `THE RIVER BOUNDARY`

# SEQUENCE VIII - THE DISTANCE BETWEEN

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - BROTHERS' ROOM - NIGHT

The mosquito nets are down.

Three pale rooms inside one dark room.

The ceiling fan turns above them. Its bearings complain once each revolution.

Outside: A basin empties. Two women finish a conversation on the veranda. A child objects to bedtime and is carried away still arguing.

The house settles around its living.

Malik lies awake. The EMPTY BRASS URN rests inside its padded case beside his bed.

Ade faces the ceiling.

Kai lies nearest the door. His camera bag sits beneath his hand.

Nobody is sleeping.

ADE
Anybody going to say it?

MALIK
Say what?

ADE
Fine.

Ade rolls onto his side.

ADE
Beautiful river. Lovely family outing.
Nobody left it terrified.

Malik looks at him through the net.

Kai does not.

KAI
You felt it.

MALIK
I felt something.

ADE
Thank you.

MALIK
That was not agreement.

A small smile almost reaches Kai.

Outside, one insect stops.

Kai's eyes move to the window.

A second call disappears farther across the compound.

Then another.

The subtraction moves toward the house.

Ade sits up.

Malik swings his feet to the floor.

The last voice on the veranda stops.

The fan continues.

Once each revolution --

its bearing complains.

The open doorway frames the passage.

At the far end: the RED-CORDED DOOR.

Moonlight enters through patterned ventilation blocks.

Four pale bands lie across the passage floor.

Kai stares.

Five.

He blinks.

Four.

KAI
Malik.

Malik is beside his net immediately.

MALIK
What?

Kai points.

The bands remain still.

Their spacing does not.

The third appears farther from the second than the passage can contain.

Ade steps down from his bed.

MALIK
Stay there.

ADE
I'm beside the bed.

Malik looks.

He is.

At the far end of the passage, beside the red-corded door --

something prevents the wall from resolving.

Not a shadow.

Not a body.

A vertical interruption in the room's depth.

Kai leans toward it.

The interruption acquires a bend.

Too high for a shoulder.

Another lower down.

The first is no longer where it was.

ADE
Kai.

Kai does not answer.

His hand rises to the back of his neck.

The passage contracts.

The red-corded door appears six feet away.

Then twelve.

Then close enough to touch.

Kai moves.

Malik catches his forearm before his foot clears the bedroom.

KAI
I wasn't --

MALIK
You were.

Kai looks down.

His toes are over the threshold.

Malik pulls him back one step.

The distance to the thing does not change.

Kai looks again.

Height gathers around it.

Too much height.

A narrow upper mass continues into the place where the ceiling should end it.

One articulation folds sideways without shortening.

Another seems nearer than the part connecting it.

Almost anatomy.

Kai forgets to breathe.

The form resolves another fraction.

Malik steps between Kai and the passage.

KAI
Move.

MALIK
No.

KAI
Malik --

MALIK
Look at me.

Kai tries to see around him.

Malik catches the other shoulder.

MALIK
Kai.

Ade moves in close behind Malik. No joke now.

ADE
Look at him.

Kai's eyes finally find Malik's.

Malik does not let go.

The fan turns.

One bearing complaint.

Then another.

Outside, an insect begins again.

Farther away than it should be.

Ade looks past Malik.

The passage is empty.

Four bands of moonlight.

The red cord at the far door.

Wall.

Nothing crosses out of sight.

Nothing leaves.

Malik releases one of Kai's shoulders.

Not the other.

MALIK
You with me?

Kai nods once.

His eyes drift toward the passage.

Malik tightens his hand.

KAI
It changed when I looked at it.

Malik follows Kai's gaze to the red-corded door.

Then down to the camera bag beneath Kai's bed.

Kai sees where he is looking.

A beat.

KAI
There's something I didn't tell you.

CUT TO:

# SEQUENCE IX - WHAT HE OPENED

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - KITCHEN - MOMENTS LATER

One burner holds a kettle below a full boil.

Bode sits at the table in a white undershirt. He has turned on one light and no more.

The brothers enter barefoot together.

Malik stays between Kai and the passage.

Ade closes the kitchen door.

Bode looks from one face to the next.

BODE
What happened?

MALIK
Show him.

Kai sets his camera on the table.

Finds the image.

The hidden shrine in the ravine.

Root and stone grown together.

Wooden cover.

Red binding intact.

Bode leans closer.

His hand stops short of the camera.

BODE
Where?

KAI
Below the western stones.

Bode looks at him.

BODE
You left the path.

KAI
I slipped after.

BODE
After.

Kai advances the image.

The red thread broken.

The recess open.

Bode stands.

BODE
Kai.

Not anger.

Worse.

Malik turns to Kai.

MALIK
What did you take?

Kai opens the camera bag.

Removes the BRASS TALISMAN.

Sets it on the table.

Bode steps back.

A bedroom door opens somewhere off the passage.

Malik does not notice.

MALIK
You carried that to the river.

KAI
Yes.

MALIK
You stood beside us with it.

KAI
Yes.

MALIK
And said nothing.

KAI
Yes.

Ade looks at the talisman.

ADE
Why keep it?

Kai searches for an answer that does not improve him.

KAI
I wanted to know what it was.

MALIK
So you took it.

KAI
I almost put it back.

MALIK
You didn't.

KAI
No.

Footsteps approach in the passage.

Bode is still looking at the open-recess photograph.

KAI
Do you know what it is?

BODE
No.

KAI
The place.

Bode takes longer.

BODE
We were told to leave that part of the land alone.

MALIK
By who?

BODE
My father. Then Solomon.

MALIK
Why?

BODE
I don't know.

Malik stares at him.

BODE
I know what happened when we stopped listening.

The kitchen door opens.

Eno stands there in a wrapper over her nightclothes.

She has heard enough to know nobody is discussing sleep.

ENO
What happened?

No one answers quickly.

She enters.

Sees the brass object on her table.

ENO
Whose is that?

KAI
I took it from the land.

ENO
From where?

Bode answers before Kai can.

BODE
Western stones.

Eno looks at Bode.

The look contains history the brothers do not have.

ENO
And he brought it here.

BODE
Yes.

Eno takes a clean dishcloth from the counter.

Drops it over the talisman without touching the brass.

ENO
Then it stays there.

She looks at Kai.

ENO
You do not carry what you do not understand through my house.

Kai nods.

Malik looks at Bode.

MALIK
You said you know what happened.

Bode lowers himself into the chair.

BODE
My sister died.

Nothing moves in the kitchen.

BODE
Solomon came home from the land without her.

Kai watches him.

KAI
Was she there?

BODE
Yes.

KAI
At that place?

BODE
I don't know.

Malik catches the distinction.

MALIK
What do you know?

Bode looks toward the passage.

BODE
Afterward, your father stopped laughing for a long time.

Ade looks down.

BODE
Then he started going back.

MALIK
To the land.

Bode nods.

BODE
And the room.

Eno's eyes move to him.

She knows exactly which room.

ADE
What room?

Eno answers.

ENO
The one at the end of the passage.

Kai looks toward the kitchen door.

MALIK
What's in it?

BODE
His records.

ENO
His work.

Bode looks at her.

ENO
Do not make it smaller because you are frightened.

Bode takes that.

MALIK
You read them?

BODE
Some.

MALIK
Enough to fix what he was doing?

BODE
No.

The answer comes without hesitation.

KAI
Enough to know if I caused this?

Bode looks at him.

BODE
Enough to know you disturbed something Solomon kept maintaining.

No more than that.

Malik reaches for the covered talisman.

Eno slaps his hand away.

Not hard.

Final.

ENO
I said it stays.

Malik looks at her.

ENO
Three of you came into this kitchen barefoot and shaking. Nobody is taking anything anywhere tonight.

She points toward the brothers' room.

ENO
Together.

Then to Bode.

ENO
Morning, you open Solomon's room.

BODE
Eno --

ENO
Before breakfast.

Ade almost smiles.

Almost.

Eno catches it.

ENO
You too.

The almost-smile disappears.

ENO
Go.

The brothers go.

Bode remains seated beneath the single kitchen light.

Eno turns off the burner.

The kettle never reaches a boil.

CUT TO BLACK.

# SEQUENCE X - THE TWO RECORDS

FADE IN:

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - PASSAGE - MORNING

Daylight fills the passage.

Children argue somewhere at the front of the house.

A radio gives the weather.

The RED-CORDED DOOR waits at the back.

Bode stands before it with a small ring of household keys.

Malik, Ade and Kai behind him.

Eno arrives carrying nothing.

The brass talisman remains in the kitchen beneath the dishcloth.

Bode touches the cord.

A simple hitch between handle and brass wall hook.

Nothing like the worked binding at the shrine.

His fingers pause.

ENO
Bode.

He undoes it.

The door is unlocked.

He opens it.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - MAINTENANCE ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Camphor.

Paper.

Machine oil.

A long worktable beneath slatted windows.

A measuring line worn dark where fingers held it.

Brass cloths blackened at the folds.

Bundles of red cotton wrapped by weight and date.

A boundary map punctured and corrected until the western edge is nearly soft.

Malik stops at the table.

Kai does not enter as far.

At the opposite wall --

AN INNER DOOR.

Older wood inside newer plaster.

Pencil measurements climb the frame beside small brass markers.

Lengths of red cord fixed at exact intervals.

Near the lower hinge, one measured point remains empty.

A brass fastener lies on the sill beneath it.

The door is still.

Kai looks at Bode.

KAI
What's behind it?

BODE
I don't know.

Malik looks over.

BODE
Solomon never opened it for me.

Eno watches the unfinished point.

ENO
Not after she died.

Bode looks at her.

Eno does not add anything.

Bode opens a cabinet.

Ledgers wrapped against humidity.

He places one on the table.

Malik opens it.

SOLOMON'S HAND.

Narrow block letters.

Dates.

Measurements.

A ruled margin corrected where the pencil drifted.

WEST BINDING — REPLACED
RIVER +11 CM
BRASS — CLEAN

Malik's thumb stops beside a knot diagram.

MALIK
Dad.

Bode nods.

Malik turns pages.

A date from years after Solomon moved to Canada.

Then another.

He looks at Eno.

MALIK
He was here.

ENO
Yes.

On another shelf, Kai sees thinner cloth-bound notebooks.

He looks at Bode.

Bode does not move.

BODE
Hers.

Kai looks to Eno.

She nods once.

He takes one.

Curved handwriting.

No columns.

Kai finds a date.

Looks across at Malik's ledger.

KAI
Same day.

Malik turns the ledger toward him.

Solomon:

WEST BINDING — REPLACED
RIVER +11 CM
BRASS — CLEAN

The sister:

Insects stopped before the rain.

Below it:

Distance on the west path changed when I looked directly.

Kai reads the sentence twice.

Ade comes closer.

Kai turns several pages.

A later entry.

Tall form beyond the palm line.

One bend where a shoulder should be.

The next did not stay in the same place.

Kai stops.

His hand rises toward the back of his neck.

He catches it halfway.

Malik sees.

No one speaks.

Kai turns the page.

Then another.

The handwriting stops halfway down one sheet.

The rest of the notebook is blank.

Malik looks back to Solomon's ledger.

The dates continue.

Page after page.

Then, months later, beneath a column of measurements:

DISTANCE UNCERTAIN.
RE-MEASURE.

Malik reads it without moving his lips.

Across the table, Kai still holds the open notebook.

Ade reaches between them.

He rotates Solomon's ledger until both books face the same direction.

Same table.

Same light.

Malik's hand remains beside Solomon's knot diagram.

Kai's beside the line about the tall form.

Ade looks from one brother to the other.

Behind them, the unfinished brass point waits on the inner door.

CUT TO BLACK.
